1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:06,792
This is DEFX Bridge. It brings a single documentation convention to both SAS and R.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,948
In this SAS program, each DEFX comment documents the code beside it. These three lines are one method, not three duplicates.

3
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:27,807
This R program uses exactly the same syntax, as R comments. Line forty-nine is a trap: a string that looks like a tag. It's code, so the tool ignores it.

4
00:00:30,570 --> 00:00:39,807
Scan reads both languages without running them. Forty-eight tag lines become forty records, each keeping its file and line numbers for traceability.

5
00:00:43,230 --> 00:00:48,627
Validation checks every target against a synthetic specification. Zero errors.

6
00:00:50,900 --> 00:01:01,119
Now a tag naming a variable that isn't in the spec. It's reported with its file name and line number, and the command exits with code two. Errors are never silently accepted.

7
00:01:04,230 --> 00:01:11,035
Export fills a copy of the workbook, reads it back, and keeps it only because all fourteen preservation checks pass.

8
00:01:15,870 --> 00:01:27,497
This is the generated file itself. The three lines are now one method description, and the hand-entered cell beside it is still there. The PreserveMe sheet is untouched, including its sentinel value.

9
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:43,217
In the template, this flag had a stale origin of Assigned. After export it's Derived, with its method. Rows without tags, like the sequence number, keep their hand-entered values.

10
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:58,679
One shared convention lets a study adopt R gradually, one program at a time. The code is on GitHub. Synthetic data only.
